//     DOL in the store order want uses (0xA8, 0xAC, 0xB0, 0xB4, 0xB8 ...),
//     one output element per statement. §6as-3: PS pair copies can be
//     written as comma expressions in the CALL/STORE argument list so the
//     scheduler interleaves psq_st with the arg setup; that is how want
//     gets its 16 stores.
//  3. The 9-store inertia block (0x800B84FC..0x800B8588) ALREADY matches
//     want value-for-value and store-SET-for-store-set; only the emission
//     ORDER differs (got: 0x16C,0x168,0x178,0x17C,0x180,0x158,0x160,0x15C,
//     0x170; want: 0x15C,0x160,0x170,0x180,0x158,0x16C,0x168,0x178,
//     0x17C). GCC here emits each store as its value becomes available,
//     not in source order, so a plain permutation will not fix it — this
//     is the same "value-availability interleave" family as §6j-6.
//  4. The GetFloat call sites still emit `mr r3, r20` where want has it
//     LAST (`... li r6, 0; mr r3, r24`). All flags and both return types
//     are already right, so this is a plain doc-15 arg-setup transposition
